# {{ app_name }}: bare-metal blinky

This app toggles the first LED in the **{{ board }}** BSP LED table every
500 ms: roughly half a second on, half a second off. The physical LED label
varies by board; it is the first entry of `am_bsp_psLEDs`, not necessarily
an LED marked "0". No FreeRTOS scheduler or SWO viewer is required.

## Build and run

```bash
nsx create-app {{ app_name }} --board {{ board }} --template blinky
cd {{ app_name }}
nsx configure
nsx build
nsx probes
nsx flash
```

With multiple probes connected, add `--probe-serial <serial>` to `nsx flash`
and `nsx reset`. After flashing, watch the board's first user LED blink.
`nsx reset` restarts the configured app without reflashing.

## Board support

The app uses the selected BSP's pin, polarity and drive-mode information
with AmbiqSuite's LED driver from the resolved SDK. It does not hardcode GPIO
numbers. Only the first LED is configured; other LEDs are left alone.

A board must provide `AM_BSP_NUM_LEDS > 0` and `am_bsp_psLEDs`. FPGA targets
and custom BSPs without this table cannot run this template; the build
reports that requirement. Use `--template default` for a SWO hello-world
app instead. Check your board's LED jumpers/routing if it builds and flashes
but no LED is visible. Build coverage alone does not verify physical LEDs.

## Next

Edit `src/main.c` to change the delay or choose another valid BSP LED index.
This app emits no console output, so `nsx view` is not needed. To try SWO,
create a separate app with `nsx create-app hello_swo --board {{ board }}
--template default`, then configure, build, flash and view that app.

`nsx.yml` owns dependencies and board selection; `modules/` and `boards/`
contain app-local dependencies. `cmake/nsx/` contains generated NSX tooling.
